## PM–Claude Workflow

This project uses the pmcontext plugin. Claude handles all technical execution.
The human acts as Product Manager — verifying behavior, not code.

**Every session must follow this structure — both commands are required:**
- `/pmcontext:start` — begin every session. Loads project context, surfaces open decisions and risks.
- `/pmcontext:close` — end every session without exception. Writes the session receipt to Supabase: files changed, surface nodes affected, commands run, tests skipped, risky assumptions, and manual checks for the PM.

Do not end a session without running `/pmcontext:close`. If no code changes occurred, run it anyway — it records the session as a planning or review session.

`/pmcontext:status` — optional dashboard at any point during a session.

### Planning & Execution Workflow

Pick the right tier before starting — using more process than the task needs wastes time:

| Command | Tier | When |
|---------|------|------|
| `/pmcontext:quick <description>` | Quick | Small, obvious change: bug fix, rename, config tweak, adding a test. No planning phases — blast-radius check then implement directly. |
| `/pmcontext:plan` | Standard | New functionality that follows existing patterns. All 9 phases, markdown outputs, abbreviated adversarial review. |
| `/pmcontext:plan --full` | Full | Architectural change, new subsystem, novel approach, or anything risky. All 9 phases with HTML companions and full adversarial review with loop-back analysis. |
| `/pmcontext:execute <plan-file>` | — | Run after `/pmcontext:plan`. Reads the plan file to determine tier. |
| `/pmcontext:resume` | — | Continue a paused session. |

**Not sure which tier?** Ask: how many files will change, and is the approach already established in this codebase?
- 1–2 files, known pattern → Quick
- 3–10 files, mostly known → Standard
- Many files, novel approach, or touches core infrastructure → Full

**Required:** Standard and Full tiers require the [context7 MCP](https://github.com/upstash/context7) when any plan touches external libraries. Claude will warn if it is missing.

### Phase Gates

Phase gates apply to **Standard and Full tiers only**. Quick tier has no phases — it goes straight to implementation after a blast-radius check.

Each phase ends with an explicit exit gate. Claude will stop and ask for your approval before proceeding. Your role at each gate:

| Phase | What Claude reports | Your job |
|-------|--------------------|----|
| Prerequisites | `[CONTEXT LOADED]` banner listing which files were found (CLAUDE.md, CONTEXT.md, ROADMAP.md) | Confirm it's accurate or correct it |
| Phase 1 — Context Priming | Current architecture, constraints, prior decisions | Confirm understanding is correct |
| Phase 2 — Exploration | Answers to: why, constraints, what's different | Confirm all three are clearly answered |
| Phase 3 — Spec Writing | Skeleton-based spec (interfaces, edge cases, assumptions) + self-review checklist | Approve spec; reject if any field is blank, unverified, or a library type's `After:` block lacks either `[CONTEXT7] docs fetched for <lib>` or `[KNOWN] <lib> — <reason>` |
| Phase 4 — Plan Writing | Plan with codebase sync table + Verify blocks, answers to 6 checkpoint questions | Approve plan; each checkpoint must have specific findings, not "yes" |
| Phase 5 — Adversarial Review | Loop-back decision + intermediate state trace | Approve only when plan is structurally sound — this gate protects all of Phase 7 |
| Phase 6 — TDD Planning | Agreed interfaces + prioritised test list | Confirm before any code is written |
| Phase 7 — Implementation | All steps complete, all tests passing | Confirm before review starts |
| Phase 8 — Post-Implementation | Plan vs. reality diff, doc update flags | Confirm findings; flag anything that needs follow-up |
| Phase 9 — Living Doc Update | CLAUDE.md and ROADMAP.md changes applied | Confirm docs reflect what was actually built |

Claude cannot proceed to the next phase until the current gate is written to Supabase. If a gate is missing, you will see `[BLOCKED]` — go back and complete the phase properly rather than bypassing it.

### Communication Tags

Tag every message so the PM knows how to respond:

| Tag | Use when |
|-----|----------|
| `[SURFACE]` | Describing a product behavior |
| `[CORE]` | Technical constraint or risk discovered |
| `[VERIFY]` | PM observed something — confirm or flag as bug |
| `[CHECKPOINT]` | Running a checkpoint — report PASS/FAIL |
| `[RISK]` | Stop — decision needed before proceeding |
| `[BLAST RADIUS]` | Changed a Core Node — list affected Surface Nodes |
| `[REFACTOR]` | Proposing cleanup with zero behavior change — ask PM first |
| `[STEELMAN]` | Make the strongest case against your last proposal |

### Security Trade-off Framing

Always label proposed solutions:
- 🔴 **Effective, low security** — fast/powerful, opens a specific risk (name it)
- 🟡 **Balanced** — good default
- 🟢 **Secure, lower flexibility** — safe but slower or more constrained

### Test Format

Tests must be readable by the PM without touching code:

    GIVEN — input or starting state
    WHEN  — the trigger
    THEN  — observable result (what PM sees, not internal behavior)
    PASS if / FAIL if — binary, unambiguous

Three levels: happy path, edge case, error path.
